# ai.wing — Change Log

Running record of every AI-made change to this project. Each entry says **what**
changed, **which files**, **why**, and **how to revert it**. Append new entries
at the top; never rewrite history.

---

## Conventions

- **Single source of truth:** `lib/content.ts`. Prices, plan names, speeds,
  hardware costs, phone number, legal copy, FAQ and nav all live there.
  No `.tsx` file contains a hard-coded price, plan name, speed, phone number
  or CTA string — verified by grep (see Entry 001, Verification).
- **To change pricing:** edit `lib/content.ts` only. Nothing else.
- **To revert a change:** follow the "Revert" line in its entry.

---

## Entry 009 — 2026-09-16 — Mobile responsiveness, GitHub push, Vercel prep

### Measured before / after (at 375px)

| | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| Horizontal overflow (320/375/768) | none | none |
| Tap targets under 44px | **19** | **0** |
| `backdrop-filter` elements | **14** | **5** (all cheap `blur-sm`) |
| Hero image frame shown on a phone | **~22%** | full portrait crop |
| Smallest body text | 10px | 12px (10px only in desktop-only markup) |

### Image art direction — the real mobile problem

A phone hero is ~0.4:1; these photographs are 1.6–1.8:1. `object-cover` was
therefore throwing away roughly three quarters of the frame's width, and the
house in the hero was simply not in shot on a phone.

Four portrait crops were generated (`*-mobile.jpg`, 1200×1500/1600) for the
hero, FAQ, How-it-works and Residential backdrops, anchored horizontally
(`ax`) so the subject survives the cut. `scripts/optimize-images.py` gained
`ax`/`ay` anchors and per-output source overrides.

New `components/ui/BackdropImage.tsx` serves them: portrait below `sm`,
landscape above. **Both variants are in the DOM** because browsers fetch
`display:none` images anyway — so the hidden one is given `sizes="…, 1px"`,
which makes Next resolve it to the smallest srcset entry. Verified at 768px:
the hidden mobile variant downloads at **1×1px**, the visible landscape at
768×432. No double download.

### Other responsive fixes

- **`backdrop-filter` gated to `sm:` and up.** It is one of the most expensive
  things you can ask a mobile GPU to do, and it was on 14 elements including
  every plan card. Phones now get opaque panels, which also read better over
  the backdrop photographs.
- **Tap targets:** header logo (28px → 44 via padding), menu button (40 → 44),
  header CTA (`h-10` → `h-11`, easing back to `h-10` from `lg`), footer links
  (16px → 44), legal links (→ 46), email link.
- **Small text:** 18 tokens made responsive — 11px→12px, 10px→11px, 9px→10px
  below `sm`. The two remaining 10px nodes are the footer logo sub-lockup and
  a badge inside the `hidden lg:block` comparison table.

### Repository

- `README.md` rewritten: architecture, image workflow, responsive contract,
  Vercel steps, and a pre-launch checklist of every placeholder.
- `.gitignore` already excluded `design/source-images/` (**108 MB**) — worth
  keeping that way, since everything under `public/` is deployed.
- Committed 47 files (largest 636 KB) and pushed to
  `https://github.com/PavithraEbbali/starlink` as `d46a557` on `main`.
  Verified: `git ls-remote` shows the commit on the remote.

### Vercel

No config needed. `next.config.ts` is empty, there are no env vars, no API
routes and no server runtime requirement beyond Next's own image optimizer.
Import the repo at vercel.com/new, accept the auto-detected Next.js preset,
deploy.

